1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:09,500
At an esports booth the line is long, the Wi-Fi is bad, and the machine runs all day.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,000
Read the call. Click the spot.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Stage 1, WARM-UP: the zone labels are on.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Stage 2, LIVE: the labels fade away. You have to know the map.

5
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:12,900
Stage 3, CLUTCH: half the calls name two spots, in order.

6
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:21,400
Your score is a reaction time.

7
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:32,900
Three initials, and your row appears on the second monitor. No server.

8
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:42,900
The next player gets a clean round, with nothing left over from the last one.

9
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:57,900
1000 rounds in one page, no reload: heap +0.21 MB, 16 DOM nodes every time, 0 errors, game work per frame 1.5 ms (p95).

10
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:13,400
WebStorm's headless inspection is a gate in npm run check: 13 runs, 49 warnings read.

11
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:27,900
1 real fix, 48 false positives switched off with a reason. Junie CLI is installed and configured.

12
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:37,900
Free to play, MIT licensed. Open it once with Wi-Fi, then it works offline.
